{"id":"1fea8809-b870-4bb1-901c-64c0fb2220da","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025262,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    required = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n    \\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Validate required symbols exist\\n    all_syms = {n['symbol'] for n in nodes}\\n    for s in required:\\n        if s not in all_syms:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {s} not present in any node')\\n    \\n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sym_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        s = n['symbol']\\n        sym_counts[s] = sym_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    # Processing order: depth descending, then node id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(),\\n                          key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    # Helper to collect subtree nodes that are still retained\\n    def subtree_retained(root_id):\\n        sub = set()\\n        stack = [root_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            nid = stack.pop()\\n            if nid in retained:\\n                sub.add(nid)\\n                stack.extend(children[nid])\\n        return sub\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove root\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        # Not removable\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Has retained children\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        # Compute subtree and its symbol counts\\n        sub_tree = subtree_retained(nid)\\n        sub_counts = {}\\n        for sid in sub_tree:\\n            s = node_map[sid]['symbol']\\n            sub_counts[s] = sub_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n        # Check that no required symbol becomes absent\\n        ok = True\\n        for req in required:\\n            current = sym_counts.get(req, 0)\\n            removed = sub_counts.get(req, 0)\\n            if current - removed <= 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        # Perform removal\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        for sid in sub_tree:\\n            retained.discard(sid)\\n            s = node_map[sid]['symbol']\\n            sym_counts[s] -= 1\\n    \\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n            'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that implements a tree reduction algorithm based on the described problem: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The input is a payload containing `nodes` (list of dicts with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and `required_symbols` (list of symbols that must be preserved). Also includes _case_nonce but likely irrelevant.\n\nGoal: produce two lists: removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted, removed_node_ids in removal order).\n\nConstraints:\n- Never remove root (id \"root\"?). Check payload: root has parent null. Must treat root as not removable even if removable field says false? Actually field says removable: false for root, so that's consistent.\n- Never remove a node that has any retained children. (A node can only be removed if all its children are also removed or not retained? Actually \"node with retained children\" means if any child is retained, cannot remove parent. So removal must be bottom-up? The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deeper first). Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is, after removal, the set of symbols present in the retained tree (including ancestors) must still contain all required symbols. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node can only be removed if it is removable (removable: true), not a required symbol (but the node's symbol may be required? The spec says \"required_symbols\" are symbols that must have at least one node with that symbol retained. So nodes that have a symbol that is required cannot be removed if it would eliminate the last occurrence of that symbol. The condition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might mean if the node itself has a symbol that is required, it cannot be removed? But careful: it says \"a required symbol\" - that could be interpreted as a node whose symbol is listed in required_symbols. But the interestingness oracle is defined by requiring those symbols to be present. So a node with a required symbol can be removed if there is another node with the same symbol that remains. But the spec says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\"? Possibly it means \"required symbol\" as a node that is marked required? But the data has no required flag; only required_symbols list. So likely condition is: a node can be removed only if after removal, every required symbol is still present in the tree. So a node with a required symbol is not inherently protected; only its removal must not cause that symbol to disappear. However the statement \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" might be interpreted as: root cannot be removed; nodes that are themselves a required symbol (i.e., their symbol is in required_symbols) cannot be removed? But that would conflict with the requirement to preserve required symbols after removal. The more common interpretation in Perses-like reduction: you can remove nodes as long as the resulting tree still satisfies the property (interestingness). Here the interestingness is defined by the required symbols. So you should not remove a node that would cause a required symbol to be lost. The phrase \"required symbol\" could be ambiguous. Let's look at the examples: In the payload, nodes include \"symbol\": \"root\", \"s1\", \"s2\", \"s0\". required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So root is required. Also s1 is required. In the tree, there are multiple nodes with symbol s1? In first case: nodes: n1 (s1), n4 (s1) - both have symbol s1. So you could potentially remove n4 if it doesn't break required symbols. But if you remove n1, then no s1 remains? Actually n4 also has s1, so s1 would still be present. So the condition is about overall presence, not per-node.\n\nBut the instruction: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" If we interpret \"required symbol\" as a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, then we could never remove n1 or n4? That would be too restrictive and not make sense. Because the algorithm is meant to remove nodes that are not necessary. The typical Perses algorithm removes nodes that are not essential for satisfying the property (interestingness). So we should allow removal of nodes with required symbols as long as the symbol remains elsewhere.\n\nBetter interpretation: The phrase \"a required symbol\" is part of the set of things you must not remove. But that seems odd. Let's check the comment from candidate_artifact: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Possibly they mean \"a required symbol\" as in a node that is marked as required (maybe some nodes have a property \"required\": true), but in these payloads, there is a `removable` field, not `required`. There is no `required` field for nodes. So likely the intended meaning is: nodes whose removal would cause a required symbol to be missing (i.e., the symbol is required and this node is the only one with that symbol) should not be removed. But the phrasing is ambiguous.\n\nGiven the typical Perses algorithm: You can delete a node (replace its subtree with a node that matches the grammar rule) but here it's simpler: we are just removing nodes? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But the payload doesn't have grammar rules. It only has nodes with parent pointers, symbols, depths. The task says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" However, we have no grammar information. So perhaps the \"grammar-valid\" part is abstracted away and we only need to enforce the constraints given: removable flag, required symbols, no removal if children retained, etc. And the decision to remove a node is based on its utility? Utility values are given but not used in the spec? The spec mentions \"utility\" in the data but not in the task. Possibly we need to decide which nodes to remove based on some maximization? Or maybe we simply remove all removable nodes that satisfy the constraints? The spec says \"Reduce\" and \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\". That suggests we attempt to remove nodes in order of depth (deeper first), and if removal is allowed (removable, not root, no retained children, and required symbols still present), then we remove it and continue. The order of removal is recorded. And retained nodes are those not removed.\n\nBut what about \"utility\"? It might be used for tie-breaking? The spec says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So at same depth, we process nodes in order of node ID (lexicographically? Probably string compare). Nothing about utility. So remove nodes in order of decreasing depth, and within same depth, ascending node ID? Or descending? Usually you process deeper nodes first. So sort nodes by depth descending, then node ID ascending (as tie-breaker). Then for each node in that order, check if it can be removed. If yes, remove it (add to removed list and also mark its parent that a child is removed? But need to update the tree state). After removal, the node's descendants are also removed (since you cannot have a subtree without the parent? The tree is a hierarchy; if a node is removed, its entire subtree is gone. So removal of a node implies all its descendants are also removed, regardless of their removable flag. That matches typical tree reduction: deleting a node removes the whole subtree. So we need to simulate removal and update the tree to reflect that children are removed.\n\nAlso \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" That means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are still present (i.e., not removed). Since we process bottom-up, children will be processed before parent. If a child is retained (not removed), then parent cannot be removed. So we need to track which nodes are retained (initially all considered retained). When we decide to remove a node, we mark it and all its descendants as removed. Also we need to check that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the retained tree.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Build a dictionary mapping node id to node info. Also build children mapping (list of child ids for each parent). Identify root (parent null).\n2. Identify required symbols set.\n3. Determine the order of processing: list of node ids, sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n4. Initialize set removed = set(), retained = set(all node ids). Also maybe maintain a set of symbols present from retained nodes (symbol presence). Initially compute symbol_presence: count of nodes per symbol that are retained.\n5. Iterate through node ids in that order. For each node id:\n   - If node id is already removed (because it's descendant of previously removed node), skip.\n   - Check removable field: if node['removable'] is False, skip.\n   - Check if node is root (parent null). Should never remove root. So skip if parent is null (or id==\"root\"? but could be other root?). Actually root is defined as node with parent null. There is only one root.\n   - Check if node has any retained children: need to see if any child id is in retained set. If yes, skip.\n   - Check if removing this node (and its entire subtree) would still preserve required symbols: compute new symbol presence after removal. For each required symbol, if the count from the node's subtree is equal to the total count of that symbol in retained set, then removing would make that symbol absent. So we need to know for each node, the set of symbols it contributes (including its own and descendants). But we can compute on the fly: For the node, we can iterate over all nodes in its subtree (including itself) that are currently retained, and subtract their contributions from current symbol_presence. If after subtraction any required symbol count becomes 0, then removal is not allowed. To efficiently compute subtree nodes, we can precompute subtree node sets using DFS from leaves. Since data is small, we can compute each time.\n   - If all checks pass, then remove: add node id to removed list, mark node and all its descendants as removed (remove from retained set), and update symbol_presence by decrementing counts for each node in the subtree that was removed.\n6. After processing all nodes, the remaining retained set is the nodes that were not removed. Sort retained ids (maybe alphabetically? The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\" - probably ascending string order). Return removed_node_ids in order of removal, retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nPotential nuance: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This should be evaluated with respect to the tree after removal, i.e., the current retained set. So we need to check that after removing the node's subtree, the required symbols are still present. That means we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol outside the subtree being removed. So we can compute, for each node, the set of symbols that appear in its subtree (among currently retained nodes). Then if any required symbol appears only in that subtree (i.e., its total count in retained set equals subtree count for that symbol), removal is not allowed.\n\nWe can compute subtree counts for each node as we go, updating as we remove. But since we process bottom-up, we can precompute initial subtree symbol counts for all nodes (including all nodes). Then as we remove nodes, we need to update counts for ancestors. Simpler: for each candidate node, we can compute the current symbol counts for the subtree by traversing the subtree (which might be removed later). Since the tree size is small (7 nodes), this is fine.\n\nEdge cases: \n- What about nodes with removable=true but they are the only ones providing a required symbol? They cannot be removed.\n- Nodes with removable=false cannot be removed, even if they are not required.\n- Nodes that become orphaned by removal of ancestors? Actually removal of a node automatically removes its descendants, so we need to skip already removed nodes.\n- Order of removal: we process in depth descending, same depth tie by node ID. That ensures bottom-up. But note: after removing a node, its descendants are removed. So those descendants will not be processed later. So we need to skip them if they are already in removed set.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build dict nodes. Each node dict has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Utility not used.\n- Build children mapping: for each node, add child to parent's list.\n- Compute set of all node ids.\n- Determine processing order: list of node ids sorted by (depth descending, id ascending). But we need node objects to get depth. So create list of node objects sorted similarly.\n- Initialize retained set to all ids, removed list empty.\n- Initialize symbol_counts: dict symbol -> number of retained nodes with that symbol.\n- Iterate sorted nodes:\n   if node.id in removed: continue (descendant already removed)\n   if node.parent is None: continue (root cannot be removed) - but also check removable field; root has removable false anyway.\n   if not node.removable: continue\n   # check children: if any child id in retained: continue\n   # compute subtree's symbol counts: get all descendant ids (including node) that are still in retained. Use BFS/DFS from node, collecting ids that are in retained.\n   subtree_nodes = [node.id] + collect children recursively that are in retained.\n   subtree_symbol_counts = symbol counts for those nodes.\n   # check for each required symbol: if symbol_counts[symbol] - subtree_symbol_counts.get(symbol,0) <= 0: removal would make that symbol absent. Actually if after removal the count would be 0. So condition: if symbol_counts[symbol] - subtree_symbol_counts.get(symbol,0) == 0 and symbol in required_symbols: can't remove.\n   # Also need to ensure that after removal, the retained set still has at least one node for each required symbol. So compute new_counts = {s: symbol_counts[s] - subtree_symbol_counts.get(s,0) for s in symbol_counts}. Then for each req in required_symbols, if new_counts.get(req,0) == 0: can't remove.\n   If allowed, then:\n      removed.append(node.id)\n      for nid in subtree_nodes:\n          removed.add(nid)\n          retained.discard(nid)\n          update symbol_counts: symbol = nodes[nid]['symbol']; symbol_counts[symbol] -= 1\n   Otherwise, continue.\n\nAfter loop:\n   retained_ids = sorted(retained) (since retained is a set)\n   output = {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nCheck: The order of removal: we add node.id at time of removal, not the subtree nodes. So removed list will have only the nodes we explicitly decided to remove (the parent node that triggered removal). That matches typical \"removed nodes\" being the nodes we choose to delete. The subtree nodes are implicitly removed but not recorded as separate removals. That seems correct.\n\nBut the spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order should be the order in which nodes are removed (the topmost nodes that are deleted). Subtree nodes are not individually \"removed\" in the sense of being a decision point. So fine.\n\nNow we need to implement this as a function `solve(case)`.\n\nThe function must return a dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nWe must not import anything beyond standard library. No randomness.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential issue: The root node has parent null. Should we treat parent null as root? Yes. Also the root node has removable false, so it's safe.\n\nAlso: depth is given, we can compute children by parent id.\n\nWe need to handle case where node id is not in nodes? Assume consistent.\n\nNow, we must output the implementation as JSON under \"implementation\" key. The response must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a string containing the Python code. The code must be escaped properly.\n\nWe need to ensure the code is correct. Let's test mentally on first fixture.\n\nFirst fixture nodes:\nroot (depth0, sym root, removable false)\nn1 (depth1, sym s1, removable false)\nn2 (depth1, sym s2, removable true)\nn3 (depth1, sym s0, removable false)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, sym s1, removable true)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, sym s2, removable false)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, sym s0, removable true)\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1.\n\nProcessing order by depth desc: depth2: n4, n5, n6. Tie-break id: n4, n5, n6. Then depth1: n1, n2, n3 (or n1, n2, n3? id order: n1, n2, n3). Then depth0: root.\n\nStart with retained all.\n\nProcess n4: removable true, children? n4 has no children (not in list). Check subtree counts: n4 symbol s1. Current symbol_counts: s1 appears in n1 (s1) and n4 (s1) = 2. After removal, s1 would be 1 (from n1). So s1 still present. Root also present. So removal allowed. Remove n4, removed=[n4], retained remove n4, symbol_counts s1-- =>1.\n\nProcess n5: removable false => skip.\n\nProcess n6: removable true, children none. Subtree symbol s0. Current s0 counts: n3(s0) and n6(s0) =2. Required symbols: root,s1 - not affected. So allowed. Remove n6, removed=[n4,n6], retained remove n6, s0 count becomes 1.\n\nNow depth1: process n1: removable false => skip.\nn2: removable true. Check children: n2 has child n4 (already removed). So does n2 have any retained children? After removal of n4, n2 has no retained children. So condition \"node with retained children\" means at least one child is retained. Here none, so allowed. Subtree of n2: includes n2 itself and its descendants that are retained (n4 not retained). So subtree_nodes = [n2] (since n4 not retained). Symbol: s2. Current symbol_counts: s2 appears in n2 (s2) and n5 (s2) =2. After removal, s2 becomes 1 (n5 still). Required symbols: root,s1 unaffected. So removal allowed. Remove n2, removed=[n4,n6,n2], retained remove n2, s2 count becomes 1.\nn3: removable false => skip.\n\nDepth0 root: not removable => skip.\n\nFinal retained: root, n1 (s1, retained children? n6 removed so no children retained?), n3 (s0), n5 (s2). So retained set = {root, n1, n3, n5}. Sorted ids: n1, n3, n5, root? Actually alphabetical order: 'n1','n3','n5','root'. So retained_node_ids = [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. Removed: [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"]. That seems plausible.\n\nCheck if removal of n2 was allowed after n4 was removed. Yes.\n\nNow check fixture case 000 output? We don't have expected output, but this seems correct.\n\nOne nuance: The \"required symbols\" list includes \"root\". The root symbol is present exactly once (root node). If we ever tried to remove the root, it's not allowed (removable false and parent null). So no issue.\n\nNow, are there any requirements about \"grammar-valid deletion\"? Since we have no grammar, we assume all deletions are valid.\n\nThus the algorithm seems to match.\n\nWe need to raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. What would be infeasible? For instance, if required_symbols includes a symbol that is not present in any node, then it's impossible to preserve it. We should raise ValueError. Also if root is missing, etc. So at start, validate that all required symbols appear in at least one node. Also ensure there is a root (node with parent null). Also ensure that for every node, parent references a node that exists, but we can assume input is valid.\n\nImplementation outline:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case.get(\"nodes\", [])\n    required_symbols = case.get(\"required_symbols\", [])\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        node_map[n[\"id\"]] = n\n        children[n[\"id\"]] = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Validate required symbols\n    all_symbols = set(n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes)\n    for s in required_symbols:\n        if s not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {s} not found in any node\")\n    # Initialize retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Build processing order\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    removed_order = []\n    # Helper function to get subtree node ids that are still retained\n    def get_subtree_retained(node_id):\n        subtree = set()\n        stack = [node_id]\n        while stack:\n            nid = stack.pop()\n            if nid in retained:\n                subtree.add(nid)\n                for child in children[nid]:\n                    stack.append(child)\n        return subtree\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:  # root\n            continue\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # Check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Get subtree retained nodes\n        subtree = get_subtree_retained(nid)\n        # Compute subtree symbol counts\n        sub_sym_counts = {}\n        for sid in subtree:\n            sym = node_map[sid][\"symbol\"]\n            sub_sym_counts[sym] = sub_sym_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        # Check required symbols after removal\n        feasible = True\n        for req in required_symbols:\n            current_total = symbol_counts.get(req, 0)\n            subtree_count = sub_sym_counts.get(req, 0)\n            if current_total - subtree_count == 0:\n                feasible = False\n                break\n        if not feasible:\n            continue\n        # Perform removal\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        for sid in subtree:\n            retained.discard(sid)\n            sym = node_map[sid][\"symbol\"]\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n            # Could be zero, but we don't remove entry; okay.\n    # After processing, retained set contains retained ids.\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nNow we need to ensure that the removal of a node does not need to consider its own symbol twice? The subtree includes the node itself, so its symbol is subtracted. That's correct.\n\nNow, about the \"node with retained children\": we check if any child is in retained. But note: after we remove descendants, a node might no longer have retained children. That is fine.\n\nNow, one nuance: The order of processing: we iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending and id ascending. But when we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree. However, later nodes that are ancestors may be processed after that removal. That's fine because they will have no retained children (since subtree removed). However, if an ancestor still has other retained children, it cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nNow, we should also consider that a node may become removable only after its children are removed? Actually we process depth-first, so children are processed before parents, so that works. But there is a subtlety: if a node has multiple children, some may be removable and some not. The removable children might be removed, making the node have no retained children, then the node itself can be considered. Since we process depth descending, after removing the removable children (same depth as node? Actually children are deeper, so processed earlier), the node will be processed later and will see no retained children. Good.\n\nNow, about the root: we explicitly skip. But what if there are multiple nodes with parent null? Not likely.\n\nNow, for the output, we need to return JSON-serializable dict. We must not mutate case.\n\nWe must raise ValueError if infeasible, e.g., required symbol missing.\n\nNow produce the final answer. The response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the escaped Python code.\n\nWe need to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". The code must be a string.\n\nI'll write the code in a compact but readable manner.\n\nEdge: In Python sorting, we use key lambda. Use '-' for reverse depth.\n\nI'll include comments? Not necessary but can.\n\nNow produce final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":6217,"total_tokens":8389,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5501},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}